rdofradio (RadioFoundry Gateway) from the package
=================================================

Installed stopped. To set it up:

1. Settings: /etc/rdofradio/config.json, a simplex hotspot on BrandMeister. Fill in the lines marked SET: your
   callsign, the frequency, BrandMeister's master, the hotspot password from SelfCare and your hotspot's ID. Until
   the password and the ID are set it runs on its own (TG 9990 plays a call back) and the web page says what's
   missing; rdofradio --config /etc/rdofradio/config.json --check-config says it too. Other setups to start from
   (a repeater, an RTL-SDR, a PlutoSDR, Tier III) are in /usr/share/doc/rdofradio/examples/. The web page can edit
   and save the settings too.
2. Options: /etc/default/rdofradio (RDOF_ARGS: --allow-tx, which a hotspot needs to start, the modem's serial port,
   --web, rdofradio --help for the rest).
3. sudo systemctl enable --now rdofradio; journalctl -u rdofradio -f shows what it does.

Anything else that holds the modem's port (MMDVMHost on Pi-Star or WPSD) has to be stopped and disabled first.

An RTL-SDR needs SoapySDR and its module, loaded only when --soapy is given:
    sudo apt install libsoapysdr0.8 soapysdr-module-rtlsdr     (Debian 10: libsoapysdr0.6, Debian 11: 0.7)
A PlutoSDR goes through libiio (installed with the package): --iio usb: or --iio ip:192.168.2.1.

The program runs as the rdofradio user, in the dialout and plugdev groups (serial ports, USB SDRs). Updates come
with apt (sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade); the web page's program update isn't used here. The service sees the
system read only but for /etc/rdofradio, /var/lib/rdofradio and /tmp; home directories are read only to it.

The web page listens on every interface by default (--web 0.0.0.0:8080 in /etc/default/rdofradio) and, like the
board's, has no login: anyone who can reach port 8080 can change the settings. To keep it to this machine, use
--web 127.0.0.1:8080 instead.
